# numpy for ci.yml's ffmpeg-validate job (tools/ci/quality_race.py and friends).
# matplotlib is for that job's spectrogram-rendering step only
# (quality_race.py's render_spectrograms(), `trend --spectrogram-dir`) - see
# that function's own docstring for why this is fine to add directly here.
#
# visqol-python is the MOS-LQO column (quality_race.py's perceptual_score() -
# see its own docstring for why ViSQOL over PEAQ and why this package over
# google/visqol, which has no PyPI wheel at all). It was deliberately held out
# of this lock when the column was first written, on supply-chain grounds: it
# is a third-party reimplementation, not Google's own, and the argument was
# that it should earn a place in every CI run rather than get one by default.
# What that deferral actually bought was a MOS column that read `n/a` in every
# published row - ~3,758 trend rows with "mos_lqo": null and every landscape
# MOS cell empty - so the perceptual half of the comparison did not exist. The
# supply-chain answer is the same one this repo already applies to every other
# Python dependency: pin it by hash, here, and review the lock diff.
#
# Pinned surface this adds beyond what numpy/matplotlib already brought in, as
# the lock diff shows it - seven packages, nothing already pinned moved
# version: visqol-python, scipy, libsvm-official, soundfile, and soundfile's
# own binding layer (cffi, pycparser, typing-extensions). The package's optional
# `accel` extra (numba) is deliberately NOT taken: it would roughly double the
# added surface to buy CI time on a step that is already bounded by
# quality_race.py's own MOS_WINDOW_S, and llvmlite/numba wheels lag new Python
# releases in a way this lock should not have to track.
#
# Regenerate the lock after any change here with:
#   python -m piptools compile --generate-hashes --strip-extras \
#     --output-file=requirements-ffmpeg-validate.txt requirements-ffmpeg-validate.in
numpy
matplotlib
visqol-python
